[[:db/retract 17592186204235 :question/answer-fields 17592186200080] [:db/retract 17592186204236 :question/answer-fields 17592186200090] [:db/retract 17592186204236 :question/answer-fields 17592186200086] [:db/retract 17592186204236 :question/answer-fields 17592186200095] {:db/id 17592186203921 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-08d898ff-f576-5e6a-bdf8-9d2e027a5e42" "question-cfc59310-e932-539a-95d8-99d9f3c66e0d" "question-7c340422-8f3e-59cd-b595-ad2e94a755e0" "question-2bcd445f-52b3-5de6-8b46-eb7aa3382e40" "question-02d6ada8-eb50-5039-9152-d73d78002d54"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203687 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{ -2,0,2 \\}.$\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (-2,-2), (-2,0), (0,0), (2,-2), (2,0) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the relation is reflexive, irreflexive, or neither." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is\n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x) \\in R$ for all $x \\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x) \\notin R$ for all $x \\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{ -2,0,2 \\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(-2,-2) \\in R, \\qquad\n(0,0) \\in  R, \\qquad\n(2,2) \\notin  R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ Since $(2,2) \\notin R$ and  $(0,0) \\in R,$ the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2cbb50f3-f95b-572e-a3ba-cdd7e80100c1" "question-5c874a7f-ef16-5ee1-ac09-75fb14ed8d90" "question-e1a27c07-b36b-5525-ba6b-847b13ebf516"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203688 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5d6068e6098da0c8164a2afe50c6b6e184366a1ecec96d4032eb35928b67285.png)\n\nWhich digraphs represent a relation, defined on the set $A = \\{\\spadesuit, \\heartsuit, \\clubsuit\\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x) \\in R$ for all $x \\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x) \\notin R$ for all $x \\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nPairs of the form $(x,x)$ correspond to the loops that start and finish at the same node in the directed graphs.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflexive nor irreflexive, we require that some nodes (vertices) have a loop and some don't. Among the given options, only digraph III has this property:\n\n![](images/a8/a834e4d6937327c01094409529596d36ac292c590d9ec9defe9d2fbac0bef983.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the nodes $\\spadesuit$ and $\\heartsuit$ have loops, while the node $\\clubsuit$ does not have a loop." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203923 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-653fba49-a6b9-5e57-b514-cf99f2424cd1" "question-7095468b-6aec-5ce6-83d1-ea12403a6b5a" "question-ccf91b35-7753-51f7-9fcf-6efffb54e68e" "question-8c3deb4f-bd86-5557-b5a7-4c0dc0f448ab"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203689 :question/problem "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $2 \\mid (y+x),$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x  \\not\\mid y ,$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $x \\: T \\: y$ if and only if $3 \\mid (x^2+y),$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x) \\in R$ for all $x \\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x) \\notin R$ for all $x \\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x) \\in R$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n2\\mid (x+x)\n$$\n\nfor any $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is irreflexive. Indeed, $(x,x) \\notin S$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx \\mid x\n$$\n\nfor any $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,1) \\notin T$ since $3 \\not \\mid (1^2+1),$ while\n\n- $(0,0) \\in T$ since $3 \\mid (0^2+0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-08d898ff-f576-5e6a-bdf8-9d2e027a5e42" :question/id #uuid "08d898ff-f576-5e6a-bdf8-9d2e027a5e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-213244" :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }.$ Given that $R$ is reflexive, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix.\n\n|  | $\\alpha $ | $\\beta $ | $\\gamma $ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\alpha $ | {{field-1}} | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $\\beta $ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |\n| $\\gamma $ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be reflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(\\alpha ,\\alpha )\\in R,\\,(\\beta ,\\beta )\\in R,\\,(\\gamma ,\\gamma )\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis means that the diagonal elements of the adjacency matrix must all be $1,$  as shown below:\n\n|  | $\\alpha $ | $\\beta $ | $\\gamma $ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\alpha $ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $\\beta $ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $\\gamma $ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e331289f-d2a2-53a6-8619-e9f3015f3a01" "answer-field-f355ce46-5c54-5aea-bacc-4b13f9e0cb5f" "answer-field-e314e494-3748-53a1-9f9a-a3e8ff76eb22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e331289f-d2a2-53a6-8619-e9f3015f3a01" :answer-field/id #uuid "e331289f-d2a2-53a6-8619-e9f3015f3a01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ab826688-22e9-5d03-a5c9-fac0134cda3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab826688-22e9-5d03-a5c9-fac0134cda3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab826688-22e9-5d03-a5c9-fac0134cda3a" :answer/id #uuid "ab826688-22e9-5d03-a5c9-fac0134cda3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f355ce46-5c54-5aea-bacc-4b13f9e0cb5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "f355ce46-5c54-5aea-bacc-4b13f9e0cb5f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dbdfeb37-93cd-58bf-9034-4c86ec249462" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dbdfeb37-93cd-58bf-9034-4c86ec249462"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbdfeb37-93cd-58bf-9034-4c86ec249462" :answer/id #uuid "dbdfeb37-93cd-58bf-9034-4c86ec249462" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e314e494-3748-53a1-9f9a-a3e8ff76eb22" :answer-field/id #uuid "e314e494-3748-53a1-9f9a-a3e8ff76eb22" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5c50f2cd-a725-5ab2-b922-726ece1440dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c50f2cd-a725-5ab2-b922-726ece1440dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c50f2cd-a725-5ab2-b922-726ece1440dc" :answer/id #uuid "5c50f2cd-a725-5ab2-b922-726ece1440dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2cbb50f3-f95b-572e-a3ba-cdd7e80100c1" :question/id #uuid "2cbb50f3-f95b-572e-a3ba-cdd7e80100c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213233" :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represent a reflexive relation defined on the set $A={-1,0,1}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nPairs of the form $(x,x)$ correspond to the loops that start and finish at the same node in the directed graphs.\n\nSince $R$ must be reflexive, we require that all nodes (vertices) have a loop. Among the given options, only the following digraph has this property:\n\n![](images/67/67e71a9121f014683271e905db7278ee6c11eeff31e707e45d70550042e44c2d.png)\n\n \n\nNote that all nodes contain a loop." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b99402b8-0fb7-59cc-98da-c0b6ae20da13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b99402b8-0fb7-59cc-98da-c0b6ae20da13" :answer-field/id #uuid "b99402b8-0fb7-59cc-98da-c0b6ae20da13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8998a9ec-e02c-584a-a952-14c1c4cb33e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-809c538a-7cf4-56dd-a4a7-a56104f37ed8" "answer-3a87d5d0-38bc-5381-9d4b-a87753999a6b" "answer-e73a3930-a703-533d-823f-716123d69016" "answer-8998a9ec-e02c-584a-a952-14c1c4cb33e2" "answer-c166310f-465b-5d9d-85cd-9046b9feec5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-809c538a-7cf4-56dd-a4a7-a56104f37ed8" :answer/id #uuid "809c538a-7cf4-56dd-a4a7-a56104f37ed8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/32/327aec97acef435e078395ac70e347d53fcaa9adbf7d1ad9c0011dd2d8a4146d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a87d5d0-38bc-5381-9d4b-a87753999a6b" :answer/id #uuid "3a87d5d0-38bc-5381-9d4b-a87753999a6b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/32/32a1efbe08709fe0e4ea4c85f062712e9949518564e96d76ab87fd7e6302c15c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e73a3930-a703-533d-823f-716123d69016" :answer/id #uuid "e73a3930-a703-533d